# Strata Repository Maintenance Plan

Below are 40 numbered sections. Each identifies a distinct component from the supplied excerpts, states its responsibilities, proposes a concrete change with a unified diff, and gives at least two verification cases. Line numbers are approximate to the supplied excerpts; implementers should anchor on the quoted context.

---

## 1. `include/strata/artifact/gguf_reader.hpp` — `GgufFile::find()` is O(n) per lookup

**Responsibilities.** `GgufFile` mmaps one GGUF shard, parses metadata and the tensor directory, and answers `find(name)` for tensor lookups. `GgufModel` already builds a `std::map` index across shards, but the single-file reader used by `strata-gguf`, `ple_parity` and the tools still linearly scans `tensors_`.

**Change.** Add a lazily built name→index map inside `GgufFile`, populated on first `find()` (the parse loop already inserts into a `std::set` for duplicate detection, so the data is available). Keep `tensors()` order unchanged.

```diff
--- a/include/strata/artifact/gguf_reader.hpp
+++ b/include/strata/artifact/gguf_reader.hpp
@@
     const TensorInfo* find(const std::string& name) const {
-        for (const auto& t : tensors_)
-            if (t.name == name) return &t;
-        return nullptr;
+        if (index_.size() != tensors_.size()) build_index();
+        const auto it = index_.find(name);
+        return it == index_.end() ? nullptr : &tensors_[(size_t) it->second];
     }
@@
     void parse() {
@@
         std::set<std::string> names;
         for (uint64_t i = 0; i < n_tensors; ++i) {
             TensorInfo t;
             t.name = c.str();
             if (!names.insert(t.name).second)
                 throw std::runtime_error("GGUF: duplicate tensor name '" + t.name + "' in " + path_);
@@
             tensors_.push_back(std::move(t));
         }
@@
     std::vector<TensorInfo> tensors_;
     std::map<std::string, MetaValue> meta_;
+    mutable std::map<std::string, uint32_t> index_;
+    void build_index() const {
+        index_.clear();
+        index_.reserve(tensors_.size());
+        for (uint32_t i = 0; i < tensors_.size(); ++i) index_.emplace(tensors_[i].name, i);
+    }
 };
```

**Verification.**
1. `gguf_reader_test` (existing CTest) still passes: duplicate-name refusal, counts (1,223/1 tensors, 202 Q2_0) unchanged.
2. New case in `gguf_reader_test`: open the tiny model, call `find()` twice for a present and an absent name, assert the returned pointer equals `&tensors()[i]` for the known index, and assert a second call after `tensors()` mutation is impossible (const API) — plus a timing assertion that 10,000 `find()` calls on the 1,223-tensor file complete in well under the old linear bound.

