## 3. `include/strata/artifact/dequant.hpp` — `dequantize_q3_K` reads `scales` past the 12-byte field

**Responsibilities.** Scalar reference dequantizers used as the oracle for every GPU kernel.

**Change.** In `dequantize_q3_K`, `aux` is 4×`uint32_t` (16 bytes) but only 12 bytes are copied from `block + 96`; the repacking then indexes `scales[is++]` up to 32 times over the 16-byte `aux` — the last 4 bytes are zero-initialized padding, which ggml also has, so the *values* are right, but the code depends on `aux` being zero-initialized (`{0,0,0,0}`) and on the loop consuming exactly 32 of the 64 scale slots. Add a static assertion-style comment and a bounds guard so a future edit that changes the loop count cannot read `aux[4]`:

```diff
--- a/include/strata/artifact/dequant.hpp
+++ b/include/strata/artifact/dequant.hpp
@@
     const int8_t* scales = (const int8_t*)aux;

     float* y = out;
     int is = 0;
+    // The two 128-element halves consume exactly 32 of the 64 int8 slots of aux[4]; the top 4 bytes are
+    // zero padding that ggml also carries.  32 * 2 halves would read aux[4] - the loop below is bounded
+    // by n < 256 (two halves x 4 j-iterations x 2 scales), which is asserted here so a future edit
+    // cannot walk off the end.
+    static_assert(sizeof(aux) == 16, "q3_K scale repack assumes a 16-byte aux");
     uint8_t m = 1; // hoisted: ggml declares this BEFORE the n loop, so it does not reset between halves
     for (int n = 0; n < 256; n += 128) {
+        if (is + 8 > 32) { std::fprintf(stderr, "dequantize_q3_K: scale index out of range\n"); std::abort(); }
         int shift = 0;
```

**Verification.**
1. `dequant_s2_parity` / `native_expert_parity` (which exercise the scalar path) unchanged on the existing fixtures.
2. A new unit case feeding a synthetic Q3_K block whose `aux` padding bytes are deliberately poisoned (memset 0xFF before the 12-byte memcpy) and asserting the output is identical to the poisoned-free run — proving the padding is never read.

